<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marriage of the Sea ceremony</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Sposalizio del Mare</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Marriage of the Sea was a maritime ceremony of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, held on Ascension Day, in which the doge threw a golden ring into the Adriatic Sea as a sign of Venice's dominion over it. According to most authors it first appeared around the year 1000, at the time of the Venetian conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> under Doge <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-ii-orseolo">Pietro II Orseolo</a>; it was fixed on Ascension Day in 1173 under Doge Sebastiano Ziani. The doge sailed out on the state galley, the bucentaur, with the Senate, and the Patriarch of Venice pronounced a nuptial blessing. It lasted until the fall of the Republic in 1797, and the mayor of Venice has re-enacted it since 1965.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/marriage-of-the-sea-ceremony.jpg" alt="Marriage of the Sea ceremony" width="330" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Francesco Guardi · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francesco_Guardi_021.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Rites of propitiation linked to the sea go back to antiquity. The French archaeologist and historian of religion Salomon Reinach recalled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polycrates">Polycrates</a>, tyrant of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samos">Samos</a>, throwing a precious ring into the sea to appease the gods, and Empress Helena casting a nail of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/true-cross">True Cross</a> into the Adriatic to make it gentler to sailors, and he held that the Venetian ceremony came from a pagan ritual taken over by the Catholic Church. Pope Alexander III confirmed its principle in 1176, during the truce in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, giving Doge Sebastiano Ziani a gold ring as the wedding ring of his marriage to the sea and asking that he and his successors marry her every year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The doge boarded the bucentaur at the Môle before the Doge's Palace in a mantle of gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> brocade with ermine, wearing the corno ducale and holding a command rod. He sat at the stern with the papal legate on his right and the French ambassador on his left, while the Senate filled four rows of seats. Off the Lido the Patriarch came aboard from another ship, and after his blessing the doge threw the ring with the words Desponsamus te, mare, in signum veri perpetuique dominii: we espouse thee, O sea, as a sign of true and perpetual dominion.</p><p>The galley's return opened the Festa della Sensa, a feast known across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> for its splendour and wealth, with a large fair in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">Saint Mark</a>'s Square. A Grand Admiral answered for the passengers' safety, since the flat-bottomed, shallow bucentaur was hard to handle and could capsize. Giacomo Casanova remarked that the slightest headwind could drown the doge with the whole government, and that Europe would then laugh that the doge had at last gone to consummate his marriage.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The art historian Sergio Bettini saw the ceremony, like the great Roman and Byzantine ceremonies, as the periodic representation and incarnation of the myth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> in real life, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> describes it as a fundamental rite of ancient Venice. In the modern re-enactment the mayor travels with the patriarch and other dignitaries on a replica of the bucentaur to San Nicolò Church, where a ring is thrown into the sea. The only surviving ring, that of an unidentified doge found by chance in the sea, is kept in the collection of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mark-the-evangelist">Saint Mark</a>'s Basilica.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Marriage of the Sea ceremony” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
